As a metaphor, that frame can be powerful: treat every person as another locus of consciousness rather than a sealed-off object. It lowers status anxiety and makes perspective-taking easier—you stop asking “How do I talk to this type of person?” and start asking “What part are they playing, what do they see, and what language reaches that position?”

And yes, communication routinely travels through other people. Reputation, stories, norms, jokes, exemplars, mutual friends, organizational culture, even somebody watching how you treat a third person—all become indirect channels. Often the strongest message is not addressed to the eventual recipient at all.

There is an important fork, though. “They don’t realize I’m doing it” can describe emergent communication—you behave consistently and the signal propagates naturally—or covert manipulation, where you deliberately conceal authorship or engineer another person’s beliefs without their informed agency. The former can create durable alignment; the latter tends to create brittle alignment because discovery changes the meaning of the entire interaction.

For the Pantheon, I’d model the cleaner version as:

speaker → medium/person → observation → interpretation → receiver → response → network feedback

And the interesting trick is that there may be no privileged speaker. You can alter the field by changing the behavior, artifact, story, incentive, or example that everybody encounters. Then people arrive at the conclusion through their own cognition rather than being secretly puppeteered toward it.

That gives you something stronger than “talking through someone”: communicating through the environment.

And once you see that, a room full of people is no longer a collection of isolated conversations. It is a coupled signaling network—every interaction changes what later interactions mean.